
  That leaves the question the whole feature turns on, and it's sharper than it first looks: [1mif you're the only person [0m
  [1mtouching your tasks, being told that you just changed something is noise.[0m "Notified when tasks they care about change"
  needs a different meaning for one user than it has for a team, or we'll build a system that mostly interrupts you
  with your own actions.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what actually generates a notification.

  - [1mTime-based (due soon / overdue)[0m — the clock is the only thing that changes without you. Needs due dates on tasks and
    a scheduler; no event history stored at all. This is where nearly all the real value is for a single user.
  - [1mActivity log of your own edits[0m — a "what changed" history you can scroll. Needs an append-only event store and a
    retention policy, and it's a permanent addition to the data model. Genuinely useful for "what was I doing Friday?",
    useless as an interruption.
  - [1mOut-of-band changes[0m — edits from another tab, or a recurring task rolling over. Small if we have the event store;
    needs cross-tab storage events.

  Cheap to add later: the activity log, if we timestamp mutations from day one. Expensive to retrofit: due dates and the
  scheduler, since they touch the task model and every render path.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m time-based reminders as the core, with mutations timestamped so an activity log is a later addition
  rather than a rewrite. That's the version that earns its interruptions.

